LCR_LEVY=lambda extra,src:('No visitor levy yet. On 10 Sep 2026 the government announced that mayors, including the Liverpool City Region Mayor, will get the power to bring in an Overnight Visitor Levy, charged as a percentage of the accommodation price. The Mayor, Steve Rotheram, has campaigned for it and says it could raise up to £18m a year across the city region (which includes '+extra+'). A bill is still to come, and the government expects mayors to set out spending plans by early 2028, so nothing applies yet and no rate, start date or exemptions have been set. Whether it will cover short-term lets as well as hotels has not been decided locally (unverified).',[EN_OVL,LCROVL]+src)
CX.update({
'liverpool':dict(
 lic=(EN_LIC2[0]+' Liverpool\'s selective landlord licensing scheme (designated wards covering about 80% of the city\'s private rented homes, 1 Apr 2022 to 31 Mar 2027) is for rented homes, not holiday lets: the national exemptions order exempts a tenancy or licence granted to someone for their occupancy of a home "as a holiday home". The council\'s own pages do not mention short lets specifically and point to the GOV.UK list of exemptions, so if a property is ever let to someone as their home (for example a longer let or a lodger arrangement) it may need a licence. Check with the council through its LAR portal or privatesector.housing@liverpool.gov.uk. HMO licensing (5 or more people from 2 or more households living there) is separate. Local reports say the council has consulted on a replacement selective licensing scheme from April 2027; we have not confirmed the outcome on the council\'s site (unverified).',[EN_REG,LVSL,LVSLX,SLX]),
 ctrl=('No short-let control area or Article 4 direction for short lets found (searched 24 Oct 2026), including in the city centre. Liverpool\'s Article 4 directions control converting homes into houses in multiple occupation (HMOs), not holiday lets: in the existing areas (the 11 Wards and The Dales directions) you need planning permission to turn a home into an HMO for 3 or more people. On 13 Oct 2026 Cabinet approved wider HMO directions (5 areas covering parts of 17 wards straight away, the rest of the city from Oct 2027), but the decision was called in on 20 Oct 2026 and is paused for now. A whole home let to one group of visitors is not an HMO, so these do not by themselves cover short lets. Whether letting a whole home short term is a material change of use is decided case by case. The council\'s draft new Local Plan (Regulation 18 draft, Sep to Nov 2025) included a planned short-term let policy (Policy TC7) on concentration, loss of family homes, noise, anti-social behaviour and waste; the Regulation 19 version was consulted on from 10 Aug to 30 Sep 2026 and has not been examined or adopted, and we have not checked whether it keeps that policy (unverified). Until then the 2022 Local Plan applies.',['https://www.gov.uk/guidance/letting-out-a-self-catering-holiday-home-in-england-rules-and-regulations',LVHMO,LVA4,LVLP,LVLP19]),
 levy=(lambda L:(L[0].replace('No visitor levy yet.','No statutory visitor levy yet. Separately, the city centre Liverpool Accommodation BID charges £2 per room or unit per night (since 1 Jun 2025), but only on hotel or serviced accommodation in the BID area with a rateable value of £45,000 or more, so a typical short let on council tax, or a small business-rated let, is not covered. The BID runs until Dec 2027.'),L[1]+[LVBID,LVBIDB]))(LCR_LEVY('Liverpool, Sefton, Knowsley, St Helens, Wirral and Halton',[LCRKS])),
 fees=('No short-let fees. Council tax: Liverpool charges twice the normal council tax on second homes (furnished, nobody\'s main home). This applies from 1 Apr 2025 under the new national power; we have not found the council\'s decision date on its site (unverified). Empty and unfurnished homes pay full council tax for the first 12 months, then a premium: 200% of the normal bill after 1 year, 300% after 5 years and 400% after 10 years, even if the owner changes. '+EN_EXC+' To claim an exception, email Revenue.Service@liverpool.gov.uk with "Empty properties and second home premium exception" in the subject and your evidence (for example the estate agent listing).'+EN_BRT,[LVCT,EN_BR]),
 dates=['1 Apr 2022: selective landlord licensing starts (holiday lets exempt)','1 Apr 2025: second homes pay double council tax','1 Jun 2025: Accommodation BID £2 room charge starts (hotels and serviced accommodation, rateable value £45,000+)','10 Sep 2026: government announces Overnight Visitor Levy powers for mayors','13 Oct 2026: Cabinet approves wider HMO Article 4 directions (called in 20 Oct)','Mar 2027: England short-term let register expected (GOV.UK)','31 Mar 2027: current selective licensing scheme ends','Dec 2027: Accommodation BID term ends']),
'sefton':dict(
 lic=(EN_LIC2[0]+' Sefton runs selective licensing in parts of Bootle and additional HMO licensing in parts of Seaforth, Waterloo, Brighton-le-Sands and Southport (both 1 Mar 2023 to 29 Feb 2028). These are for privately rented homes; the national exemptions order exempts a tenancy or licence granted to someone for their occupancy of a home "as a holiday home", so a genuine holiday let should not need a selective licence. Sefton\'s pages do not mention short lets specifically, so if you let rooms separately or let to people as their home, check with landlord.licensing@sefton.gov.uk.',[EN_REG,SFSL,SFAL,SLX]),
 ctrl=('Planning guidance applies. Sefton adopted a Conversions to Short Term Holiday Lets Supplementary Planning Document on 5 Dec 2024 (in operation from 18 Dec 2024). It says a home let as a whole to one family-sized group, with no more occupants than would normally live there, is unlikely to need planning permission. Letting rooms separately to different groups, adding bedrooms, or letting to a group larger than a normal household (or a large all-adult group) is likely to be a material change of use that needs permission. Converting shops, offices, pubs and other non-residential buildings always needs permission. In Primarily Residential Areas the council will refuse a conversion if 5% of properties within 100 metres are already short-term lets, flats or HMOs, and expects no more than 2 people per room, no subdivided rooms, enough parking and bin space, and no hot tubs or outdoor bars that could disturb neighbours. Town, district and local centres are favoured. A let of more than 28 days at a time is treated as an ordinary home or HMO, not a holiday let. Separately, Article 4 directions now cover the whole borough for converting homes into small HMOs (most recently the rest of Sefton from 15 Oct 2025); these are about HMOs, not short lets.',[SFSPD,SFSPDA,SFA4]),
 levy=LCR_LEVY('Sefton, so Southport, Formby and Crosby',[LCRKS]),
 fees=('No short-let fees. Council tax: on 18 Jan 2024 Full Council approved a 100% premium on homes that are substantially furnished with nobody living there, so second homes pay 200% council tax from 1 Apr 2025. Empty and unfurnished homes pay a premium after 1 year: 200% of the normal bill for 1 to 5 years, 300% for 5 to 10 years and 400% after 10 years, regardless of change of owner. '+EN_EXC+' Sefton also lists Class M (empty homes needing major repairs, 12 months). To claim, email Council.Tax@sefton.gov.uk with your account number in the subject and the class you are claiming; for a home on the market, give the date marketing started and where it is advertised.'+EN_BRT,[SFCT,SFMIN,EN_BR]),
 dates=['1 Mar 2023: selective (Bootle) and additional HMO licensing start','18 Jan 2024: Full Council approved the second homes premium','18 Dec 2024: short-term holiday lets planning guidance (SPD) in force','1 Apr 2025: 100% second homes premium started','15 Oct 2025: HMO Article 4 direction extended to the rest of Sefton','10 Sep 2026: government announces Overnight Visitor Levy powers for mayors','Mar 2027: England short-term let register expected (GOV.UK)']),
})
